  "summary": "One blue, patented, total: a color as a finished artwork.",
  "description": "International Klein Blue (IKB) is less a pigment than an artwork: the matte, velvet-deep ultramarine Yves Klein developed with chemist Edouard Adam (suspended in a synthetic resin binder that preserved raw pigment luminosity) and registered under a Soleau envelope in 1960. Klein showed it as monochrome canvases, sponges, and the living-brush \"Anthropométries\" — color stripped of composition until the hue itself carries the event. IKB escaped the gallery: design, fashion, album covers (Derek Jarman's Blue cinema), and every \"Klein blue\" sneaker quote it. The aesthetic lesson: total commitment to one color can be maximalism, not minimalism.",
  "culturalContext": "IKB is the color as artwork: Klein's resin-suspended ultramarine (Soleau-registered 1960) made monochrome commitment a radical act; the blue escaped into design, fashion and music, and remains the reference for single-hue maximalism in art and brand culture.",
